//! `sos` — the SOS userspace API (design 140; sos/spec.md §2, §5.7, §12).
//!
//! U-MODE LIBRARY CODE, OWNED AND EXPORTED BY THE KERNEL PACKAGE. Nothing here
//! runs in kernel mode; all of it lives in the kernel's tree. That is the
//! Zircon-vDSO discipline (ratified Aug 5) and it buys one specific thing:
//! SYSCALL NUMBERS ARE NOT ABI. The op numbers come from `sosabi`, which the
//! kernel's dispatch tables import too, so the two halves of the contract
//! cannot skew — and because a process links this module instead of writing
//! numbers, the kernel may renumber between builds without breaking anything
//! that was compiled against it.
//!
//! THREE ENTRY ALTITUDES, ONE IMPLEMENTATION CHAIN. There is exactly one place
//! in the system that issues a trap, and everything above it is a wrapper:
//!
//!   typed Saw   `system.shutdown(0)`                 <- prefer this
//!   typed C     `sos_system_shutdown(h, 0)`          <- for non-Saw languages
//!   raw         `sos_syscall1(h, op, arg)`           <- the HAL stub
//!
//! The Saw methods below call the `@export`ed C functions, which call the raw
//! form, which is the per-arch `ecall`/`svc` stub in `sos/hal/<arch>/user/`.
//! No altitude reimplements the one below it, so there is no second trap path
//! to keep in step.
//!
//! WHAT A PROCESS MUST STILL AGREE WITH THE KERNEL ABOUT: the calling
//! convention (§5.7 — handle in a0, op in a7, status back in a0) and the status
//! tags it branches on. The first lives in the HAL stub; the second is
//! `SosStatus` below. Neither is a number this file's callers ever write.
//!
//! THE TWO LAYERS, AND WHICH ONE CARRIES A GUARANTEE (ruled Aug 15, user). The
//! TYPED SAW SURFACE NEVER EXPOSES OR ACCEPTS A RAW HANDLE WORD: `System`,
//! `Process` and `Thread` keep private fields, offer no word extractor and no
//! word constructor, and every one of them is minted INSIDE this module by the
//! op wrapper that received it from the kernel — so the only crossing INTO the
//! typed layer is `System(boot_handle:)`, §12's root of trust, and there is no
//! crossing out at all. A handle you hold is one the kernel handed you, and a
//! handle you were never given is not a thing you can spell.
//! The `@export`ed C SURFACE below is the FLOOR and keeps raw `UInt` handles,
//! because a C caller has no type system to carry the guarantee in. That is a
//! documented difference in kind, not an oversight: the C altitude buys a
//! non-Saw process the same operations with none of the same protection, and a
//! process that wants the protection writes Saw.
//!
//! WHICH ENUMS ARE PUBLIC API, AND WHICH ARE BOOKKEEPING (ruled Aug 16, user).
//! The vDSO discipline is about OP NUMBERS, not about argument encodings, and
//! the line runs exactly there:
//!
//!   PUBLIC — an enum or record whose values a PROCESS CHOOSES and passes as a
//!   syscall argument, or that the kernel HANDS BACK as data. `EventMode` is
//!   the first kind: a caller names it (`event_create(mode:
//!   EventMode.SaturatingSum)`), so it is API, and the alternative — a method
//!   per mode — reads fine at two and stops scaling at the next object, where
//!   M3's Mapping brings an access mode AND a memory type and a method per
//!   combination is a matrix rather than a list. `WaitTag` / `WaitPayload` /
//!   `WaitResult` and the wait record's word offsets are the second kind: the
//!   kernel WRITES that record and userspace READS it, so its layout is a
//!   contract between the two and one declaration has to serve both. Mapping
//!   inherits both patterns.
//!
//!   KERNEL-INTERNAL — op numbers, rights bits, `ObjType`, `FaultReason`, the
//!   dispatch's bookkeeping. A process never writes one of these, which is what
//!   leaves the kernel free to renumber between builds.
//!
//! EXACTLY ONE DECLARATION EITHER WAY, and for a public one that declaration
//! stays in `sosabi` — the kernel's dispatch decodes the same enum, and a second
//! copy here is precisely the skew that file exists to prevent. Saw's imports
//! RE-EXPORT, so the one declaration is reachable through this module and a
//! process writes `import sos.{EventMode}`. It is worth knowing that the
//! re-export is INDISCRIMINATE — everything imported here is reachable through
//! here, and `sosabi` is on a process's module path as a transitive dependency
//! regardless — so the split above is held by what userspace is TOLD to write
//! rather than by a wall. That was already true; the rule only says which side
//! of the line each family sits on.
//!
//! (The other direction was probed and does not work: the enum cannot MOVE here
//! and be imported by the kernel, because this module and the kernel HAL export
//! the two runtime hooks under the same C names for their two sides — importing
//! one into the other is a duplicate-`@export` error, since a Saw import
//! compiles the whole module into the unit.)
//!
//! # THIS FILE IS THE FACADE (sawos design 5)
//!
//! The module used to be one 2,000-line file. It is now twelve modules and this
//! facade, one file per object, and this file holds no code: it re-exports the
//! names a process writes, so `import sos.{System}` in `root/src/main.saw` and
//! every `import sos.{...}` in `tests/*/src/main.saw` means exactly what it
//! always did.
//!
//! THE MODULES, IN DEPENDENCY ORDER — a file may import only those above it,
//! because an import cycle is a compile error naming the loop (DF-232e):
//!
//!   `floor`      the two syscall externs, the status split, the whole
//!                `@export`ed C-ABI surface, `ALL_RIGHTS`, the owning tier's
//!                contract, and the boot handle the runtime hooks print
//!                through
//!   `memory`     Memory — RAM authority, and `split`
//!   `iomemory`   IoMemory — device authority, and `carve`
//!   `mapping`    Mapping — one installed protection row, and `unmap`
//!   `pipe`       PipeInlet + PipeOutlet — the two ends of a connection, and
//!                `post` / `take` — plus, since M4 unit 2, PipeReply +
//!                PipeRequest, the one-shot pair one exchange is made of, and
//!                `resolve` / `reply`. FOUR TYPES IN ONE FILE, alone among
//!                these, because they are TWO PAIRS and every sentence about
//!                either half of either pair explains it by contrast with its
//!                opposite. Nothing forced it: no type here names another
//!                except at `take`'s return, so the module graph was
//!                indifferent and READABILITY chose — which is the placement
//!                rule that became available when sawlang 0.2.0 made extension
//!                lookup follow `public import` (SL-7)
//!   `thread`     Thread
//!   `event`      Event
//!   `interrupt`  Interrupt
//!   `timer`      Timer
//!   `clock`      Clock, which constructs Timer
//!   `waiter`     Waiter, whose three `add` overloads name the three above
//!   `system`     System, Process and BootHandle — see below
//!   `rt`         `sos_rt_write` / `sos_rt_abort`, both written over a System
//!                op
//!
//! WHERE THE LINE FELL, twice, and neither is where the brief's sketch put it:
//!
//!   - `waiter` MOVED ABOVE the three waitables. `add` is one overload per
//!     kind (`&Event`, `&Interrupt`, `&Timer`) and each overload reads the
//!     target's handle field, so the aggregator names all three and none of
//!     them names it. Written below them it would be the cycle DF-232e
//!     diagnoses.
//!   - `system` HOLDS THREE TYPES, because every pair of them names the
//!     other: System constructs Process, `Process.give(system:)` consumes a
//!     System, and `BootHandle` carries all three while `Process` answers with
//!     one. `system.saw`'s own header has the full argument. There is no
//!     `process.saw`; a type's extension methods must live in the module that
//!     declares the type (a re-export widens no extension scope — design
//!     142/229), so no arrangement of files splits them today.
//!   - **THE SAME FINDING BIT AGAIN IN M3 UNIT 4, and it moved a METHOD rather
//!     than a file.** Design 6 sketched the map funnels as `Memory.map(into:
//!     &Process)`; `BootHandle` carries a `Memory?` and an `IoMemory?`, so both
//!     of those modules are BELOW `system` and a `map` written in either naming
//!     `Process` is the cycle DF-232e diagnoses. So the funnels are
//!     `Process.map(memory:access:)` and `Process.map(iomemory:)`, which reads
//!     exactly like the `give` overloads beside them — the same receiver
//!     answering the same question about the same child — and the region is
//!     BORROWED rather than consumed, because a map moves no handle word.
//!   - **AND A THIRD TIME IN M3 UNIT 5.5, at the one place the brief had
//!     written the other spelling out.** Design 8 ruled the surface
//!     `Waiter.add(process:, key:)` — the fourth member of the overload list
//!     `waiter.saw` already holds. It is unwritable here: `waiter` is ABOVE the
//!     three waitables precisely because `add` reads its argument's handle
//!     field, and `Process` is declared in `system` one line further up, so the
//!     fourth overload would import downward into its own importer. So it is
//!     `Process.attach(waiter:, key:)`, unit 4's move applied verbatim — the
//!     receiver flips, the semantics do not, and `waiter.saw` carries a comment
//!     where the overload would have gone so the list still reads as four. The
//!     three sites together are the same missing language tier (an `internal`
//!     or forward-declaration story, or extension lookup that follows
//!     `public import`); until it exists, method PLACEMENT in this package is
//!     decided by the module graph rather than by which receiver reads best.
//!   - **THE TIER LANDED (sawlang 0.2.0, the first pin bump — SL-7 closed):**
//!     extension lookup follows `public import` now, so an `extension` beside
//!     the type it cannot be declared with is visible wherever the facade is
//!     imported. The third site is respelled to the ruled surface —
//!     `Waiter.add(process:, key:)` lives in `system.saw` as an `extension
//!     Waiter`, `Process.attach` is retired, and `waiter.saw`'s raw funnel
//!     widened to `public(package)` for exactly that one caller. The first
//!     two sites STAND: this fix moves methods, not declarations, so the
//!     System/Process/BootHandle module split and the `Process.map` receiver
//!     flip keep their recorded reasons (the map funnels also READ well —
//!     nobody has argued them back).
//!
//! WHAT A SPLIT COSTS HERE: the names two seams share are `public(package)`,
//! which is a REAL package-internal tier here rather than kcore's DF-232f
//! widening to bare `public`, because `kernel/sysapi/Saw.toml` is a manifest
//! root and a manifest root IS a package. So the NINE wrapper handle fields a
//! sibling constructs or reads — Memory, IoMemory, Mapping (M3 unit 4), Thread,
//! Event, Interrupt, Timer, Clock, Waiter — are reachable by their siblings and
//! by nothing outside the package, and `System`'s and `Process`'s stay private
//! because only their own module touches them. Nothing widened to bare
//! `public`, and the surface below is exactly what it was plus what unit 4
//! added.

// THE ENCODINGS A PROCESS IS MEANT TO WRITE OR MATCH ON, and the only things
// this module re-exports (design 229): the status tag every `Result<_,
// SosStatus>` hands back, the mode a `event_create(mode:)` caller names, and
// the record + payload a `wait()` caller matches on. `import sos.{SosStatus}`
// (etc.) is how a process takes them.
//
// **AND M4 UNIT 3 ADDED FIVE MORE TO THAT SECOND KIND** (sawos design 15;
// `designs/010` rulings 8 and 11(a)). `OutletLevel`, `InletLevel` and
// `ReplyDelivery` are what the four new `WaitPayload` cases carry, `PipeMsg` is
// the body a reply delivery hands over — the shared struct unit 4's
// message deliveries will reuse — and `ReplyLevel` is the discriminator the
// decode reads. Every one of them is written by the kernel and read by
// userspace, which is the rule's own definition of the kind, and a process
// cannot match on a payload whose sub-enum it cannot name.
public import sosabi.{SosStatus, EventMode, ClockType, WaitResult, WaitPayload,
                      BootHandleKind, OutletLevel, InletLevel, ReplyLevel,
                      ReplyDelivery, PipeMsg}

// **AND `MapAccess`, WHICH `Process.map` MADE PUBLIC** (sawos design 6 D-3).
// It is the module docstring's PUBLIC kind exactly: a value a PROCESS CHOOSES
// and passes as a syscall argument. It is also the case the docstring's own
// `event_create` note PREDICTED — "M3's Mapping brings an access mode AND a
// memory type and a method per combination is a matrix rather than a list" —
// and the prediction held one way and not the other: the ACCESS is a value a
// caller names, and the MEMORY TYPE turned out to be a KIND (`IoMemory`) rather
// than an argument, so `map(iomemory:)` takes no mode at all.
public import sosabi.{MapAccess}

// **AND `SystemRight`, WHICH THE MINT MASK MADE PUBLIC** (the Aug-29 rulings on
// design 4). Rights bits were kernel-internal for as long as no argument
// carried one; `MINT_OP` takes a KEEP MASK the caller assembles, so the bits are
// now exactly what the module docstring's rule calls PUBLIC — values a PROCESS
// CHOOSES and passes as a syscall argument. `System.mint(rights:)` is the one
// consumer, which is why this is the one rights enum re-exported: a kind whose
// handles nothing masks has no reason to publish its bits, and each one joins
// this line with the funnel that needs it.
//
// It does NOT breach the vDSO wall, which is about OP NUMBERS: userspace still
// never writes an op, and a right is a capability rather than an index the
// kernel trusts.
public import sosabi.{SystemRight}

// **AND `MemoryRight`, WHICH THE EXEC GATE MADE PUBLIC** (sawos design 9 D-1).
// The same rule again, and the sentence above says which funnel brings a rights
// enum here: `Memory.mint(rights:)` is a caller assembling a keep mask out of
// these bits, so they are values a PROCESS CHOOSES and passes as a syscall
// argument. What forced it is `MemoryRight.MapExecute` — before it, a launcher
// had no reason to narrow a region at all (`Split` and `Map` are what a region
// is FOR), so the bits had no consumer and stayed kernel-internal; withholding
// the authority to map executable is the first policy anybody writes about RAM.
//
// It publishes NO op number, exactly as `SystemRight` publishes none: a right is
// a capability the caller already holds, not an index the kernel trusts.
public import sosabi.{MemoryRight}

// **AND THE PAIR'S THREE, WHICH M4 UNIT 1 MADE PUBLIC** (sawos design 13). The
// same rule a third and fourth time — a rights enum joins this list with the
// funnel that needs it, and `PipeInlet.mint(rights:)` / `PipeOutlet.mint(rights:)`
// are callers assembling a keep mask out of these bits. That is where a
// launcher's pipe policy is written: an endpoint's default set has to be
// permissive (attenuation is monotonic, so a bit not minted at creation could
// never appear later), so "this child may post and may not hand the end on" is a
// mask at the mint and nothing else.
//
// **AND THE ONE-SHOT PAIR'S TWO, WHICH M4 UNIT 2 MADE PUBLIC** (sawos design
// 14), on the same rule and with a sharper case for it than any before:
// `PipeRequest.mint(rights:)` through a mask of `Transfer | Mint` describes a
// COURIER — a delegate that may pass an obligation further along §2.1's
// forwarding chain and may never speak for it — which is a policy the ratified
// zero-copy example genuinely wants to be able to state.
//
// `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` is here for a different reason and it is the plainest one in
// the file: it is a SIZE THE TWO HALVES MUST AGREE ON. A `post` may not name
// more, a `take` must supply at least that much, and the typed methods take a
// `&[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES]` — so a caller cannot even declare the buffer
// without the number. It publishes no op and gates no authority; it is the same
// kind of thing `wait_record_bytes()` is, and it is the one build define of the
// unit that crosses the boundary (the staging ring's DEPTH stays in
// `kcore.limits`, because a caller meeting a full ring is told `WouldBlock`
// without ever needing to know how deep it was).
public import sosabi.{PipeInletRight, PipeOutletRight, PipeReplyRight,
                      PipeRequestRight, PIPE_BODY_BYTES}

// **AND `ProcessRight`, WHICH DESIGN 16 MADE PUBLIC** — the rule a fifth time,
// and the funnel that needed it arrived in the same unit: `Process.mint(rights:)`
// is a caller assembling a keep mask out of these bits, and it exists because
// `ProcessRight.Stats` is a bit whose whole point is to be WITHHELD. "You may
// learn my child died, and you may not watch it work" is a policy a supervisor
// states once at a mint; without the enum on this line it would be a policy
// nobody outside the kernel could spell.
//
// It publishes no op number, exactly as the four enums above publish none.
public import sosabi.{ProcessRight}

// Everything else `sosabi` holds is KERNEL-INTERNAL and stays that way: the op
// numbers, the rights, the handle aliases, the wait record's wire layout. The
// modules below import what each of them needs directly, and every one of those
// is an ordinary import, so a process cannot reach one through `sos` under any
// spelling — the vDSO discipline ("syscall numbers are not ABI") is a wall now
// rather than a convention the module docstring asks callers to respect. The
// split narrowed that wall rather than widening it: a file names the handful of
// encodings it uses instead of the whole list.

// The object modules, and the C-ABI floor under them. Re-exported by name
// rather than by glob: the list IS this module's surface, so a name that is not
// here is not something a process can reach, and adding one is a decision with
// a line to write.
public import sos.floor.{ALL_RIGHTS, sos_handle_mint, sos_handle_release,
                         sos_system_debug_print, sos_system_shutdown,
                         sos_system_process_self, sos_system_process_create,
                         sos_system_clock_get,
                         sos_process_thread_create, sos_process_thread_self,
                         sos_process_exit, sos_process_get_status,
                         sos_process_event_create, sos_process_waiter_create,
                         sos_process_interrupt_bind, sos_process_start,
                         sos_process_give, sos_process_boot_handle_next,
                         sos_thread_start, sos_thread_join, sos_thread_exit,
                         sos_thread_yield,
                         sos_event_signal, sos_event_receive,
                         sos_waiter_add, sos_waiter_remove, sos_waiter_wait,
                         sos_interrupt_ack,
                         sos_clock_now, sos_clock_timer_create,
                         sos_timer_arm, sos_timer_disarm,
                         sos_memory_split, sos_memory_map,
                         sos_iomemory_carve, sos_iomemory_map,
                         sos_mapping_unmap,
                         sos_process_pipe_create, sos_pipe_inlet_post,
                         sos_pipe_outlet_take, sos_pipe_reply_resolve,
                         sos_pipe_request_reply}
public import sos.memory.{Memory}
public import sos.iomemory.{IoMemory}
public import sos.mapping.{Mapping}
public import sos.pipe.{PipeInlet, PipeOutlet, PipeReply, PipeRequest}
public import sos.thread.{Thread}
public import sos.event.{Event}
public import sos.interrupt.{Interrupt}
public import sos.timer.{Timer}
public import sos.clock.{Clock}
public import sos.waiter.{AttachMode, Waiter}
// `ProcessStats` joins the three types this module has always published, and it
// is the one that is not a capability: it is the ANSWER `Process.stats()` gives
// (sawos design 16), three plain counts a process may hold, copy and print. A
// name on this line is a name a process can reach, and the reading has to be one
// — a method whose return type could not be spelled is a method nobody can bind.
public import sos.system.{System, Process, ProcessStats, BootHandle}

// THE ONE NON-RE-EXPORTING LINE, and it is here for a reason no `public import`
// can serve: `sos.rt` publishes nothing — its two functions are module-private
// and reach the world through `@export` alone — so the facade has no name to
// re-export and still has to name the module, because an import is what
// compiles it into the unit. Without this line a process links with no
// `sos_rt_write` and its first `print` goes nowhere.
import sos.rt
